From d23b873abd45240509c1f02a4e3d54a8ede80732 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:32:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09934/11362] Name where a receipt was read from, and raise
 when git itself fails

Three follow-ups on REQ-REPORT-6610, two from the team lead and one from an
independent reviewer. Each is proved by mutation, and each mutation asserts its
anchor first: ruff had reflowed a multi-line raise in an earlier pass, so a
mutation string silently matched nothing and an inert mutation read exactly like
a guarded one.

1. A GIT FAILURE NO LONGER READS THE DISK. `artifact_commit` treated a non-zero
`git log` and "this artifact never landed" as the same answer: an empty commit
id, which sends the receipt to the working tree. A broken git would therefore
have turned every receipt in the corpus into a working-tree hash while still
calling itself a commit receipt -- trusted and silent, the state this project
holds to be worse than no guard. `git log` failing now raises. A checkout with
no commits at all is still authoring, checked by `rev-parse --verify HEAD`
before the log, so an empty repository does not raise.

2. THE FOUR WORKING-TREE CASES ARE NOW DISTINGUISHABLE, and the distinction is
asserted rather than commented. `receipt_source()` returns `commit`,
`live_evidence`, `authoring_never_committed` or `outside_checkout`. Three of
those read the working tree and previously returned an identical value, so a
deliberate live read of an evidence path looked the same as a receipt that could
not be resolved. The authoring branch is fail-open ON PURPOSE -- refusing it
would stop a new experiment writing its first receipt -- and that is now stated
in the code and in REQ-REPORT-6610-AUTHORING-FAIL-OPEN, not inherited.

3. A DECORATIVE TEST, FOUND BY AN INDEPENDENT REVIEWER.
`test_req_report_6610_tamper_is_still_detected` asserted only

    receipt != _sha("a value nobody ever hashed\n")

which stays true when the hash function is replaced by a constant. The hash was
guarded, but by the churn test, not by the test named for it. It now also
asserts the expected digest; the constant-hash mutation takes that test alone
red. This is the project's own "prove each pattern is under test by deleting it"
rule, applied to the suite of the requirement that states it.

TWO LIMITS WRITTEN INTO THE REQUIREMENT rather than left to be rediscovered.

Weakening a requirement is unguarded. A sibling replay test pins the capability
spec with `spec.index("## REQ-LEARN-<id>")`, so deleting the section raises and
the test fails, while changing a `SHALL` to a `MAY` inside it passes. Confirmed
here, not taken on report, against REQ-LEARN-5342-2: deletion RED, weakening
GREEN. In those terms -- "the live spec still contains this requirement" is
guarded; "it still says the same thing" is guarded by nothing. Commit-resolution
neither causes this nor fixes it: the old working-tree receipt did move on a
weakening edit, but it moved on every unrelated edit too, so it never
distinguished them.

A pinned receipt cannot see a later edit to what it covers. For `results/` that
is why REQ-REPORT-6610-EVIDENCE-LIVE exists. It still applies to every other
pinned path, including an upstream `.py` module named in a receipt.
`_EVIDENCE_PREFIXES` holds `results/` alone; widening it is the lever.

Adoption completeness was swept, not assumed: every replay-tested module was
scanned for a line still hashing a repository file from the working tree.
Exactly two remain, and both are the deliberate non-adoptions already named with
reasons. The scan keys on the file-read primitive rather than an aggregator
name, which is why three aggregator names and four primitive names in the corpus
are covered by one rule.

Mutation proofs, each RED then GREEN on restore: constant hash; git-failure
fall-through; empty-repo authoring guard removed; evidence branch mislabelled as
authoring. 21 helper tests pass. The 68 replay tests are unchanged at
13 failed / 65 passed / 1 error, identical red set.

